      "quote": "The photo identification requirement is repealed.",
      "mechanism": "Removes the requirement for voters to present a government-issued photo ID or cast a provisional ballot, simplifying the steps needed to cast a regular ballot.",
      "certainty": "stated"
    },
    {
      "id": "f2",
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "concern_id": "C59",
      "effect": "costs",
      "direction": null,
      "quote": "The photo identification requirement is repealed.",
      "mechanism": "Removes the step of verifying a voter's identity using a government-issued photo ID before providing a ballot at a polling place.",
      "certainty": "stated"
    }
  ],
  "analysis": [
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Current law requires voters to present a government-issued photo ID to receive a regular ballot at a polling place, or cast a provisional ballot while providing the state with a free ID option. The measure changes this by eliminating the photo ID requirement entirely, instead asking voters to state their name and address and sign the poll book.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "answer": "The state would likely save money previously spent on producing and issuing free identification cards to registered voters. Voters without IDs save the time, transportation, and opportunity costs associated with acquiring one.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "Voters who lack a photo ID will be able to vote more easily at polling places. Polling place lines may move faster due to simplified check-in procedures. Public perception of election security may change depending on voter trust in the signature and poll-book verification process.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "answer": "The measure addresses the barrier to voting that ID requirements pose to individuals who lack them. By repealing the requirement, the measure simultaneously removes the primary mechanism previously used to prevent in-person voter impersonation.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q7",
      "answer": "The impact of voter ID requirements is empirically disputed. Some researchers find that strict ID laws suppress voter turnout, particularly among minority, low-income, and older voters who are less likely to possess qualifying IDs. Other researchers find that ID laws have minimal or no measurable effect on overall or minority turnout. Similarly, while proponents argue ID laws are necessary to prevent fraud, most empirical studies find that in-person voter impersonation is extremely rare.",
      "disputed": true
    }
  ],
